  <Groups>
    <Group elementId="CPLX-7044" textLabel="CPLX-7044" type="Complex">
      <Xref identifier="CPLX-7044" dataSource="plantcyc" />
      <Graphics centerX="150" centerY="395" width="140" height="100" textColor="666666" fontName="Arial" fontSize="10" borderColor="9900CC" borderStyle="Dashed" borderWidth="2.0" fillColor="F5E6FF" shapeType="Rectangle" zOrder="-1" />
      <Comment>Serine palmitoyltransferase catalyzes the first committed and plausible rate-limiting step in sphingolipid biosynthesis. The enzyme has been characterized from a wide range of organisms including yeast, mammals, and plants. In yeast and human, the enzyme is a heterodimer of LCB1 and LCB2 subunits and each subunit is encoded by a single copy gene|CITS: [Lynch04]|. Arabidopsis contains one homolog of LCB1 and two homologs of LCB2. The LCB2 gene can functionally complement the yeast lcb2 mutant and result in the synthesis of sphingolipids. Like the LCB2 subunit in other organisms, the Arabidopsis LCB2 protein contains a highly conserved pyridoxal phosphate binding domain. AtLCB2 is expressed in leaf, stem, root and flower but low in mature seeds|CITS: [11726713]|. The Arabidopsis LCB1 homolog has not been functionally characterized.</Comment>
      <Property key="UniqueID" value="CPLX-7044" />
